#!/usr/bin/env python3
# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — drives on the desktop, and no app icons on the desktop
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS DOES, IN TWO SENTENCES
#   1. Every drive that is mounted gets an icon on your desktop — including the
#      drive AquariusOS itself is installed on, which is labelled "AquariusOS",
#      exactly the way a Mac shows "Macintosh HD". Double-click one and the
#      Files app opens on that drive.
#   2. The desktop is kept for files and drives only. An application icon that
#      lands on it is quietly moved into a holding folder, not deleted.
#
# It is a small program that runs in the background for each logged-in person.
# It costs almost nothing: it sleeps until the kernel tells it something has been
# mounted or unmounted, and wakes for a moment every two seconds to glance at
# whether the desktop folder changed.
#
# ==============================================================================
# PART 1 — WHY DRIVES ON THE DESKTOP NEEDS A PROGRAM AT ALL
# ==============================================================================
# On a Mac this is a checkbox. On KDE Plasma 6 there is no such checkbox, and no
# hidden setting either — the feature genuinely does not exist. What Plasma gives
# us instead is a desktop that IS a folder: the icons you see are the real files
# in your Desktop folder, drawn by a component called Folder View.
#
# So the way to put a drive on the desktop is to put a FILE in the Desktop folder
# that stands for that drive. That is what this program does, and it is the only
# mechanism that needs nothing compiled and nothing installed from outside.
#
# THE FILE WE WRITE, and why each piece of it is the way it is:
#
#     [Desktop Entry]
#     Type=Link                 ← "I am a shortcut to a place", not a program.
#     Name=Game Drive           ← what is written under the icon
#     Icon=drive-harddisk       ← the picture
#     URL=file:///run/media/system/Game Drive
#     X-Aquarius-Volume=true    ← our own marker; see below
#
#   Type=Link is doing a lot of quiet work. KDE follows a Link shortcut straight
#   to its destination with no "are you sure you want to run this?" warning and
#   no need for the file to be marked executable — unlike a shortcut to a
#   PROGRAM, which is checked carefully before it is allowed to run. So
#   double-clicking a drive icon simply opens the drive.
#     Source: kio/src/gui/openurljob.cpp — handleDesktopFiles() calls runLink()
#     for `cfg.hasLinkType()` before any of the executable-file checks.
#
#   The file name ends in `.desktop` and it has to. KDE only reads the `Name=`
#   line out of a file whose name ends that way, so a differently-named file
#   would show up on the desktop with its raw filename underneath instead of the
#   drive's proper name.
#     Source: kconfig KDesktopFile::isDesktopFile() is literally
#     `return path.endsWith(QLatin1String(".desktop"));`, and plasma-workspace
#     kioworkers/desktop/kio_desktop.cpp calls it before reading `Name=` into
#     the label shown on the desktop.
#
#   X-Aquarius-Volume=true is a marker with no meaning to KDE at all. It is how
#   THIS program recognises its own files. It is the reason we can tidy up
#   drives that have been unplugged without ever risking one of your files: we
#   only ever delete a file that carries our mark.
#
# WHAT WE DELIBERATELY DID NOT DO
#   * No third-party desktop widget. The spec for this desktop
#     (docs/gnome-principles-kde-spec.md, item B7) rules out compiled add-ons,
#     and every "volumes on desktop" widget for Plasma is one.
#   * No symbolic links instead of these shortcut files. A link to a folder gets
#     a plain folder icon, and we want real drive icons with real drive names.
#
# ==============================================================================
# PART 2 — WHY "NO APP ICONS" IS A TIDY-UP AND NOT A LOCK
# ==============================================================================
# Royce's rule: like a Mac, the desktop holds files and drives, never
# applications. The obvious way to deliver that would be to switch off the
# "Add to Desktop" menu item. We went and checked whether Plasma lets us, and the
# honest answer is that it does not — not cleanly.
#
#   The menu item is offered whenever ONE condition is true: the desktop is not
#   locked. There is no setting, and no system-administrator override, that
#   turns off just this one thing.
#     Source: plasma-workspace/applets/kicker/actionlist.cpp line ~152 —
#       if (ContainmentInterface::mayAddLauncher(appletInterface, ...Desktop))
#           actionList << ... i18n("Add to Desktop") ...
#     and plasma-workspace/applets/kicker/containmentinterface.cpp,
#     mayAddLauncher(), whose entire test for the Desktop case is
#       return (containment->immutability() == Plasma::Types::Mutable);
#
#   The two things that WOULD stop it are both locked doors:
#     * locking the desktop (`plasma/plasmoshell/unlockedDesktop` in KDE's
#       administrator settings) — which also stops you moving any icon,
#       rearranging any widget, or editing your app launcher's favourites;
#     * making the Desktop folder read-only, which KDE's own documentation
#       suggests and which would also stop you saving a file to your desktop.
#       Source: https://develop.kde.org/docs/administration/kiosk/keys/ —
#       "In order to prevent adding, removing, or renaming icons, you should set
#        the desktop folder read-only."
#   AquariusOS does not ship locked doors (spec item B7: "GNOME's 'simple' means
#   simple defaults, not locked doors"), so neither of those is acceptable.
#
#   Hiding all shortcut files instead was considered and rejected: Folder View's
#   file filter can hide every `.desktop` file, but our drive icons ARE
#   `.desktop` files, so that setting would hide the drives too. There is no way
#   to write the filter so that it catches one and not the other — its patterns
#   have no way to say "except these".
#     Source: plasma-desktop containments/desktop/plugins/folder/foldermodel.cpp
#     — filterAcceptsRow() hides an item when
#       !(matchPattern(item) && matchMimeType(item))
#     and matchMimeType() compares the file's real type, which for every shortcut
#     file, ours included, is `application/x-desktop`.
#
# SO: A TIDY-UP, WITH THE FRONT DOOR LEFT UNLOCKED.
#   Nothing stops you putting an app icon on the desktop. This program simply
#   picks it up again and puts it in a holding folder —
#   ~/.local/share/aquarius/desktop-app-icons/ — and tells you it did. Nothing
#   is deleted, the app itself is untouched and still in the app grid, and the
#   file can be dragged straight back out of the holding folder.
#
#   And it is one line to switch off, which is written into the notification you
#   get and repeated in the holding folder's own README. See CONFIG below.
#
# ==============================================================================

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import re
import select
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from urllib.parse import quote

# The file the drive shortcuts and the tidy-up both answer to. It does not exist
# by default; creating it is how somebody changes their mind.
CONFIG_PATH = "~/.config/aquarius-desktop.conf"

# Where an application icon goes when it is tidied off the desktop.
HOLDING_DIR = "~/.local/share/aquarius/desktop-app-icons"

# The line in our shortcut files that means "this one is ours".
OUR_MARKER = "X-Aquarius-Volume=true"

# Put this line in any shortcut on your desktop and the tidy-up will leave it
# exactly where it is, forever. It is the per-file escape hatch, next to the
# per-machine one in ~/.config/aquarius-desktop.conf.
KEEP_MARKER = "X-Aquarius-Keep-On-Desktop=true"

# ==============================================================================
# THE SHORTCUTS THIS PROGRAM MUST NEVER MOVE  (added 2026-08-30)
# ==============================================================================
# This block exists because the first version of this program broke the single
# most important thing about the handheld edition of AquariusOS, and it did it
# within about two seconds of the owner logging in.
#
# HERE IS THE STORY, because the reasoning matters more than the list.
#
# On a handheld (our aquarius-os-deck image, built on bazzite-deck) the machine
# normally starts straight into Steam's Game Mode. You leave Game Mode for the
# ordinary desktop by choosing "Switch to Desktop" in Steam. That choice STICKS:
# it is written into the automatic-login settings, and the machine keeps landing
# on the desktop every time it is switched on until something writes those
# settings back.
#   Source: ublue-os/bazzite, /usr/libexec/bazzite-autologin — it writes
#   /etc/sddm.conf.d/zz-holo-autologin.conf ONLY `if [[ ! -f ${HOLO_CONF} ]]`,
#   so once another program has written that file, a reboot does not undo it.
#
# The one thing that writes it back is a shortcut sitting on the desktop called
# "Return to Gaming Mode" (~/Desktop/Return.desktop). Bazzite puts it there and
# puts it BACK if it goes missing.
#   Source: ublue-os/bazzite, /usr/libexec/bazzite-user-setup —
#   `if [[ $IMAGE_NAME =~ "deck" ]]` ... `cp /etc/skel/Desktop/Return.desktop`.
#   The file itself comes from the steamdeck-kde-presets package and reads
#   `Type=Application`, `Exec=/usr/bin/return-to-gamemode`.
#
# And `Type=Application` is exactly what the tidy-up below looks for. So we
# picked up the one icon that leads home and filed it in a hidden folder. On a
# handheld with no keyboard and no mouse attached, that is not an inconvenience —
# it is a dead end. The machine looks, to its owner, like it has stopped booting
# into Game Mode.
#
# So: a shortcut that CHANGES WHICH SESSION THE MACHINE STARTS IN is not an app
# icon. It is part of how the computer works, it stays on the desktop, and if a
# previous version of this program already moved one, we put it back (see
# restore_protected_launchers below).
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Matched on the file name, case-insensitively. Bazzite's own scripts refer to
# this file by name, so the name is the most stable thing about it.
PROTECTED_LAUNCHER_FILENAMES = {
    "return.desktop",
}

# Matched anywhere in a shortcut's `Exec=` line. These are the commands that
# switch the machine between Game Mode and the desktop; every one of them was
# read out of the bazzite-deck image rather than guessed at.
#
#   /usr/bin/return-to-gamemode        the Return icon's own command
#   steamosctl switch-to-game-mode     what that command actually runs
#   steamos-session-select             Valve's name for it, kept for Steam
#   os-session-select                  Bazzite's copy of the same script
#   org.kde.Shutdown.logout            the older Return icon shipped by Valve,
#                                      which logs out and lets automatic login
#                                      take you back to Game Mode
SESSION_SWITCH_COMMANDS = (
    "return-to-gamemode",
    "steamosctl",
    "steamos-session-select",
    "os-session-select",
    "org.kde.Shutdown.logout",
)

# ==============================================================================
# WHICH SESSIONS THIS PROGRAM RUNS IN  (added 2026-08-30)
# ==============================================================================
# Same story as above, from the other end. This program draws icons on a Plasma
# desktop. Game Mode is not a Plasma desktop — it is Steam running on its own
# compositor, with no desktop folder, no icons and nothing for us to do.
#
# It is switched on for every account with `systemctl --global enable`, and a
# handheld's Game Mode session does start the same `graphical-session.target`
# that pulls us in, so without this check we would run there and quietly poke at
# a Desktop folder nobody can see. Nothing good can come of that, so we don't.
#
# HOW THE CHECK IS WRITTEN, and why it leans the way it does: a session says
# what it is in the XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP environment variable, which Plasma sets
# to "KDE" and Valve's Game Mode sets to "gamescope".
#   Source: the desktop-entry specification, §"Registered OnlyShowIn
#   Environments"; plasma-workspace startplasma sets XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=KDE and
#   hands it to systemd with `dbus-update-activation-environment --systemd`.
#
# If that variable is EMPTY we run anyway. That is deliberate and it is the safe
# direction to fail in: an empty answer means we could not tell, and the cost of
# running where we should not is a couple of unread files, while the cost of NOT
# running where we should is the feature silently doing nothing on a desktop PC.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DESKTOP_SESSION_NAMES = ("kde", "plasma")


def session_is_a_desktop() -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Should this program run in the session it has been started in?

    Returns the answer and a sentence saying why, so the journal explains itself.
    """
    current = (os.environ.get("XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP") or "").strip()
    if not current:
        return True, "no session name was set, so we assume an ordinary desktop"
    names = [part.strip().lower() for part in current.split(":") if part.strip()]
    if any(any(known in name for known in DESKTOP_SESSION_NAMES) for name in names):
        return True, f"this is a Plasma desktop session (XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP={current})"
    return False, (
        f"this is not a desktop session (XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP={current}) — on a "
        f"handheld that means Game Mode, which has no desktop to draw on"
    )


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH MOUNTED THINGS COUNT AS "A DRIVE"
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linux mounts a great many things that are not drives: bookkeeping folders the
# kernel invents, temporary folders that live in memory, the innards of Flatpak
# apps. None of those belong on a desktop. Rather than trying to list every kind
# of non-drive, we ask two questions, and something has to pass BOTH:
#
#   1. Is it backed by a real piece of hardware? (its source starts with /dev/)
#   2. Is it somewhere a person would expect a drive to be?
#
# The second question is what the list below answers.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVE_LOCATIONS = (
    # Where this OS, Bazzite, and systemd all put fixed disks.
    "/run/media/",
    # Where KDE puts a card or a USB stick you plugged in: /run/media/<you>/...
    # (covered by the line above), and where older tools put them.
    "/media/",
    # The traditional "mount it here" folder. On this kind of OS /mnt is really a
    # signpost pointing at /var/mnt, so both spellings are listed.
    "/mnt/",
    "/var/mnt/",
)

# Filesystems that exist only inside the computer's head — no disk, nothing to
# show. Listed as a second net in case something ever gets mounted into one of
# the folders above without being a real drive.
NOT_A_DRIVE_FSTYPES = {
    "autofs", "bpf", "cgroup", "cgroup2", "configfs", "debugfs", "devpts",
    "devtmpfs", "efivarfs", "fuse.gvfsd-fuse", "fuse.portal", "fusectl",
    "hugetlbfs", "mqueue", "nsfs", "overlay", "proc", "pstore", "ramfs",
    "securityfs", "selinuxfs", "sysfs", "tmpfs", "tracefs",
}


def expand(path: str) -> str:
    return os.path.expanduser(path)


def log(message: str) -> None:
    """Write a line where `journalctl --user -u aquarius-desktop-volumes` sees it."""
    print(message, flush=True)


# ==============================================================================
# CONFIG — the two switches, and how somebody turns them off
# ==============================================================================
# ~/.config/aquarius-desktop.conf, if it exists, is read as plain `key=value`
# lines. Both switches are ON unless the file says otherwise:
#
#     ShowDrivesOnDesktop=false        stop putting drive icons on the desktop
#     KeepAppIconsOffDesktop=false     stop tidying application icons away
#
# It is a file rather than a System Settings page because Plasma has no page to
# put it on — these are behaviours Plasma does not have, so there is no existing
# checkbox to hang them off. The file is checked every time round, so a change
# takes effect within a couple of seconds with nothing to restart.
# ==============================================================================
def read_config() -> dict[str, bool]:
    settings = {"ShowDrivesOnDesktop": True, "KeepAppIconsOffDesktop": True}
    try:
        with open(expand(CONFIG_PATH), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
                    continue
                key, _, value = line.partition("=")
                key = key.strip()
                if key in settings:
                    settings[key] = value.strip().lower() not in ("false", "no", "0", "off")
    except OSError:
        pass  # No file is the normal case: both switches stay on.
    return settings


def desktop_dir() -> str:
    """Find this person's Desktop folder, in their language.

    On a machine set up in French the folder is called "Bureau", not "Desktop".
    The name is recorded in ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs, which is the standard
    place every desktop on Linux agrees to look.
      Source: https://www.freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/xdg-user-dirs/
    """
    path = None
    try:
        with open(expand("~/.config/user-dirs.dirs"), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                line = line.strip()
                if line.startswith("XDG_DESKTOP_DIR="):
                    raw = line.split("=", 1)[1].strip().strip('"')
                    path = os.path.expandvars(raw.replace("$HOME", "~"))
                    path = expand(path)
    except OSError:
        pass

    if not path:
        path = expand("~/Desktop")

    os.makedirs(path, exist_ok=True)
    return path


# ==============================================================================
# READING THE LIST OF MOUNTED DRIVES
# ==============================================================================
def mount_table(path: str = "/proc/self/mountinfo") -> list[dict[str, str]]:
    """Read /proc/self/mountinfo — the kernel's live list of what is mounted.

    (`path` is only ever given a different value by the test suite, which feeds
    it a saved copy of a real machine's mount table. Nothing else passes it.)

    Each line looks like this, and the fields we want are marked:

      36 35 98:0 / /run/media/system/Games rw,noatime - ntfs3 /dev/sda2 rw,uid=1000
      |      |      |                              |         |
      |      |      mount point (field 5)          |         source device
      |      major:minor device number (field 3)   filesystem type
      mount id

    The awkward part of the format is that the number of fields is not fixed:
    between the mount options and the filesystem type there can be any number of
    extra tags, and a lone "-" marks where they stop. So we split on that.
      Source: Linux kernel Documentation/filesystems/proc.rst, §3.5
    """
    rows: list[dict[str, str]] = []
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
            content = handle.read()
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"could not read the list of mounted drives: {error}")
        return rows

    for line in content.splitlines():
        if " - " not in line:
            continue
        left, _, right = line.partition(" - ")
        left_fields = left.split()
        right_fields = right.split()
        if len(left_fields) < 5 or len(right_fields) < 2:
            continue
        rows.append(
            {
                "devnum": left_fields[2],
                # Mount points are written with spaces escaped as \040.
                "mountpoint": left_fields[4].replace("\\040", " ").replace("\\011", "\t"),
                "fstype": right_fields[0],
                "source": right_fields[1].replace("\\040", " "),
            }
        )
    return rows


def label_map() -> dict[str, str]:
    """Build a "device file → the name the drive calls itself" lookup.

    Linux keeps a folder of signposts, /dev/disk/by-label/, with one entry per
    named drive pointing at that drive's device file. Reading the folder
    backwards gives us every drive's own name in one go, with no extra programs
    to run.
    """
    names: dict[str, str] = {}
    for folder in ("/dev/disk/by-label", "/dev/disk/by-partlabel"):
        try:
            entries = os.listdir(folder)
        except OSError:
            continue
        for entry in entries:
            try:
                target = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(folder, entry))
            except OSError:
                continue
            # by-label wins over by-partlabel because the filesystem's own name
            # is the one a person actually typed; a partition label is usually
            # something a tool made up.
            if target not in names or folder.endswith("by-label"):
                names[target] = unescape_device_name(entry)
    return names


def unescape_device_name(name: str) -> str:
    r"""Turn `Game\x20Drive` back into `Game Drive`.

    The signposts in /dev/disk/by-label/ cannot have spaces or other awkward
    characters in their names, so Linux writes those as `\x` followed by the
    character's number. Undoing that has to be done on the raw BYTES rather than
    on the text, because an accented letter is written as two of those codes and
    only means anything once both are put back together.
    """
    data = re.sub(
        rb"\\x([0-9a-fA-F]{2})",
        lambda match: bytes([int(match.group(1), 16)]),
        name.encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape"),
    )
    return data.decode("utf-8", "replace")


def is_removable(source_device: str) -> bool:
    """Is this drive one you can pull out — a card, a stick, an external disk?

    Linux writes a 1 into a file called `removable` for such a device. The file
    belongs to the whole DRIVE, not to a partition of it, so for /dev/sda1 we
    have to walk up to /dev/sda first — which sysfs lets us do just by looking at
    the folder above.
      Source: Linux kernel Documentation/ABI/stable/sysfs-block
    """
    name = os.path.basename(source_device)
    candidates = (
        f"/sys/class/block/{name}/removable",
        f"/sys/class/block/{name}/../removable",
    )
    for candidate in candidates:
        try:
            with open(candidate, encoding="ascii") as handle:
                return handle.read().strip() == "1"
        except OSError:
            continue
    return False


def icon_for(mountpoint: str, fstype: str, source: str) -> str:
    """Pick the picture for this drive.

    Every name below is a real icon in Breeze, the icon set AquariusOS uses, so
    none of these can come out as a blank square.
      Source: KDE/breeze-icons, icons/devices/
    """
    if mountpoint == "/":
        return "drive-harddisk-root"
    if fstype in ("iso9660", "udf"):
        return "media-optical"
    if source.startswith("/dev/mmcblk"):
        return "media-flash-sd-mmc"
    if is_removable(source):
        return "drive-removable-media-usb"
    return "drive-harddisk"


def drives(
    rows: list[dict[str, str]] | None = None,
    labels: dict[str, str] | None = None,
) -> list[dict[str, str]]:
    """The list of drives that should have an icon on the desktop right now.

    (Both arguments exist so the test suite can hand this function a saved mount
    table from a real machine instead of the one it is running on. In normal use
    neither is passed and both are read live.)
    """
    if labels is None:
        labels = label_map()
    if rows is None:
        rows = mount_table()
    found: dict[str, dict[str, str]] = {}

    for row in rows:
        mountpoint = row["mountpoint"]
        source = row["source"]
        fstype = row["fstype"]

        if not source.startswith("/dev/"):
            continue
        if fstype in NOT_A_DRIVE_FSTYPES:
            continue

        # "/" is the drive AquariusOS itself lives on. It is included on purpose:
        # this is the "Macintosh HD" icon, and it is the one Royce specifically
        # asked for.
        is_root = mountpoint == "/"
        if not is_root and not mountpoint.startswith(DRIVE_LOCATIONS):
            continue

        if is_root:
            name = "AquariusOS"
        else:
            name = labels.get(os.path.realpath(source)) or os.path.basename(mountpoint)

        # One icon per physical drive. The system drive in particular is mounted
        # in several places at once on this kind of OS (at /, at /var, at
        # /sysroot); only the first, shortest one earns an icon.
        key = row["devnum"]
        if key in found and len(found[key]["mountpoint"]) <= len(mountpoint):
            continue

        found[key] = {
            "name": name.strip() or os.path.basename(mountpoint),
            "mountpoint": mountpoint,
            "icon": icon_for(mountpoint, fstype, source),
        }

    return sorted(found.values(), key=lambda drive: (drive["mountpoint"] != "/", drive["name"]))


# ==============================================================================
# WRITING (AND CLEANING UP) THE DESKTOP ICONS
# ==============================================================================
def shortcut_text(drive: dict[str, str]) -> str:
    """The contents of one drive's shortcut file.

    `[Desktop Entry]` is deliberately the very first line: the desktop-entry
    standard requires that group to come first, and our explanation is put at the
    bottom instead, where a comment is unambiguously allowed.
      Source: https://specifications.freedesktop.org/desktop-entry-spec/

    `quote()` on the mount point matters more than it looks. This line is read as
    a web-style address, so a drive called "Rock & Roll" or one with a space in
    its name would otherwise produce an address that stops at the first awkward
    character and opens the wrong folder.
    """
    return (
        "[Desktop Entry]\n"
        "Type=Link\n"
        f"Name={drive['name']}\n"
        f"Icon={drive['icon']}\n"
        f"URL=file://{quote(drive['mountpoint'])}\n"
        f"{OUR_MARKER}\n"
        f"X-Aquarius-Volume-MountPoint={drive['mountpoint']}\n"
        "\n"
        "# Written automatically by AquariusOS.\n"
        "#   /usr/libexec/aquarius-desktop-volumes\n"
        "# Deleting this file is fine — it comes back next time the drive is\n"
        "# mounted. To stop drive icons appearing at all, put this one line in\n"
        "# ~/.config/aquarius-desktop.conf:\n"
        "#   ShowDrivesOnDesktop=false\n"
    )


def safe_filename(name: str) -> str:
    """Turn a drive's name into something that can be a filename."""
    cleaned = "".join("-" if character in "/\\\0" else character for character in name)
    cleaned = cleaned.strip().lstrip(".").strip()
    return f"{cleaned or 'Drive'}.desktop"


def is_ours(path: str) -> bool:
    """Did WE write this file?

    This is the safety catch that the whole program leans on. Only a file
    carrying our marker line is ever deleted or replaced, so there is no path
    through this code that can touch one of somebody's own files. The reading is
    capped at a sensible size so that pointing this at, say, a 4 GB video file
    that happens to be sitting on the desktop cannot swallow all the memory.
    """
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
            return OUR_MARKER in handle.read(64_000)
    except OSError:
        return False


def desktop_entry_type(path: str) -> str | None:
    """Read the `Type=` line out of a shortcut file, if it has one."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                line = line.strip()
                if line.startswith("Type="):
                    return line.split("=", 1)[1].strip()
    except OSError:
        return None
    return None


def is_protected_launcher(path: str, filename: str | None = None) -> bool:
    """Is this shortcut one that must stay on the desktop no matter what?

    Three ways to qualify, any one of which is enough. See the long explanation
    at the top of this file under "THE SHORTCUTS THIS PROGRAM MUST NEVER MOVE".

      1. Its file name is one Bazzite's own scripts look for by name.
      2. Its `Exec=` line runs one of the commands that switch the machine
         between Game Mode and the desktop.
      3. Somebody put our keep-me line in it by hand.

    Anything we cannot read is treated as protected. Refusing to move a file we
    could not open is the cautious answer, and it costs nothing.
    """
    if (filename or os.path.basename(path)).lower() in PROTECTED_LAUNCHER_FILENAMES:
        return True
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
            text = handle.read(64_000)
    except OSError:
        return True
    if KEEP_MARKER in text:
        return True
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line.startswith("Exec="):
            continue
        command = line.split("=", 1)[1]
        if any(known in command for known in SESSION_SWITCH_COMMANDS):
            return True
    return False


def restore_protected_launchers(desktop: str) -> None:
    """Put back anything an older version of this program should never have moved.

    This is the repair half of the 2026-08-30 fix. A machine that already ran the
    first version has its "Return to Gaming Mode" icon sitting in the holding
    folder, and on a handheld with no keyboard that is the difference between
    "my games are one tap away" and "I cannot get back". Updating the OS has to
    FIX that, not merely stop doing it — so on every start we look in the holding
    folder for anything protected and walk it back out.

    A file already on the desktop is never overwritten; in that case the stashed
    copy is simply left where it is for the owner to look at.
    """
    holding = expand(HOLDING_DIR)
    try:
        entries = sorted(os.listdir(holding))
    except OSError:
        return  # No holding folder is the normal, happy case.

    for entry in entries:
        if not entry.endswith(".desktop"):
            continue
        source = os.path.join(holding, entry)
        if not os.path.isfile(source) or not is_protected_launcher(source, entry):
            continue
        destination = os.path.join(desktop, entry)
        if os.path.exists(destination):
            continue
        try:
            shutil.move(source, destination)
            log(f"put a system shortcut back on the desktop: {entry}")
        except OSError as error:
            log(f"could not put {entry} back on the desktop: {error}")


def sync_drive_icons(desktop: str, enabled: bool) -> None:
    """Make the desktop's drive icons match the drives that are actually there."""
    wanted: dict[str, str] = {}
    if enabled:
        used: set[str] = set()
        for drive in drives():
            filename = safe_filename(drive["name"])
            # Two drives really can share a name. Number the second one rather
            # than letting one silently replace the other.
            stem, extension = os.path.splitext(filename)
            suffix = 2
            while filename in used:
                filename = f"{stem} ({suffix}){extension}"
                suffix += 1
            used.add(filename)
            wanted[filename] = shortcut_text(drive)

    try:
        existing = os.listdir(desktop)
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"could not read the desktop folder: {error}")
        return

    # Remove the icons for drives that are no longer mounted. The `is_ours`
    # check is the safety catch: a file we did not write is never touched, so
    # there is no way for this to eat somebody's work.
    for entry in existing:
        if entry in wanted:
            continue
        path = os.path.join(desktop, entry)
        if os.path.isfile(path) and is_ours(path):
            try:
                os.remove(path)
                log(f"removed the desktop icon for a drive that is gone: {entry}")
            except OSError as error:
                log(f"could not remove {entry}: {error}")

    # Add or refresh the icons for drives that are mounted. Files are only
    # rewritten when their contents would actually change, so the desktop is not
    # churning away rewriting the same five files forever.
    for filename, text in wanted.items():
        path = os.path.join(desktop, filename)
        try:
            with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
                if handle.read() == text:
                    continue
        except OSError:
            pass
        try:
            with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
                handle.write(text)
            log(f"put a drive icon on the desktop: {filename}")
        except OSError as error:
            log(f"could not write {filename}: {error}")


HOLDING_README = """\
Application icons that were tidied off the desktop
==================================================

AquariusOS keeps the desktop for files and drives only, the way macOS does.
When an application shortcut lands on your desktop it is moved in here instead.

Nothing has been deleted, and the app itself is untouched — it is still in the
app grid (click the AquariusOS logo in the top bar) and still in your dock.

To put one back on the desktop for good, first turn this behaviour off by
putting this single line in the file ~/.config/aquarius-desktop.conf:

    KeepAppIconsOffDesktop=false

...then drag the shortcut back out of this folder onto your desktop. The change
takes effect within a couple of seconds; there is nothing to restart.
"""


def notify(title: str, body: str) -> None:
    """Show a desktop notification, if this machine can.

    Never allowed to be a problem: if notify-send is missing or the desktop is
    not listening, we shrug and carry on.
    """
    if not shutil.which("notify-send"):
        return
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            ["notify-send", "--app-name=AquariusOS", "--icon=drive-harddisk", title, body],
            check=False,
            timeout=10,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass


def tidy_app_icons(desktop: str) -> None:
    """Move application shortcuts off the desktop into the holding folder.

    Four kinds of file are deliberately left alone:
      * anything that is not a `.desktop` shortcut — your actual files;
      * our own drive icons (they carry the X-Aquarius-Volume marker);
      * shortcuts to a PLACE rather than a program (`Type=Link`) — a bookmark to
        a website or a folder is a file, not an app, and stays;
      * shortcuts that are part of how the machine works rather than apps —
        above all "Return to Gaming Mode" on a handheld. See
        is_protected_launcher() and the long note at the top of this file.
    """
    try:
        entries = sorted(os.listdir(desktop))
    except OSError:
        return

    moved: list[str] = []
    for entry in entries:
        if not entry.endswith(".desktop"):
            continue
        path = os.path.join(desktop, entry)
        if not os.path.isfile(path) or is_ours(path):
            continue
        if desktop_entry_type(path) != "Application":
            continue
        if is_protected_launcher(path, entry):
            continue

        holding = expand(HOLDING_DIR)
        try:
            os.makedirs(holding, exist_ok=True)
            readme = os.path.join(holding, "README.txt")
            if not os.path.exists(readme):
                with open(readme, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
                    handle.write(HOLDING_README)

            destination = os.path.join(holding, entry)
            # Never overwrite something already in there.
            stem, extension = os.path.splitext(entry)
            suffix = 2
            while os.path.exists(destination):
                destination = os.path.join(holding, f"{stem} ({suffix}){extension}")
                suffix += 1

            shutil.move(path, destination)
            moved.append(entry)
            log(f"moved an app icon off the desktop: {entry} -> {destination}")
        except OSError as error:
            log(f"could not tidy {entry} off the desktop: {error}")

    if moved:
        names = ", ".join(name[: -len(".desktop")] for name in moved)
        notify(
            "The desktop is for files and drives",
            f"{names} was moved to Home ▸ .local ▸ share ▸ aquarius ▸ "
            f"desktop-app-icons. The app itself is untouched — it is still in "
            f"the app grid. To keep app icons on the desktop instead, see the "
            f"README in that folder.",
        )


# ==============================================================================
# THE LOOP
# ==============================================================================
def run_once(desktop: str) -> None:
    settings = read_config()
    if settings["KeepAppIconsOffDesktop"]:
        tidy_app_icons(desktop)
    sync_drive_icons(desktop, settings["ShowDrivesOnDesktop"])


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # `--check-session` is what the service file asks BEFORE starting us, using
    # systemd's ExecCondition=. Answering 0 means "yes, run"; answering 1 means
    # "no, skip this one" — and systemd treats that as skipped rather than
    # broken, so nothing turns red in `systemctl --user status`.
    #   Source: systemd.service(5), ExecCondition= — "if an exit code other than
    #   0 or 255 is returned ... the remaining commands are skipped and the unit
    #   is *not* marked as failed."
    # --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    if "--check-session" in argv:
        wanted, why = session_is_a_desktop()
        log(f"{'running' if wanted else 'skipping'}: {why}")
        return 0 if wanted else 1

    # The same check again, for anybody who starts this program by hand. It is
    # cheap and it means the answer cannot depend on which way you started it.
    wanted, why = session_is_a_desktop()
    if not wanted:
        log(f"nothing to do: {why}")
        return 0

    desktop = desktop_dir()

    if "--once" in argv:
        restore_protected_launchers(desktop)
        run_once(desktop)
        return 0

    log(f"watching drives and the desktop folder at {desktop}")
    restore_protected_launchers(desktop)
    run_once(desktop)
    last_desktop_change = _mtime(desktop)

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # HOW WE WAIT WITHOUT BURNING BATTERY
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # /proc/self/mountinfo is a special file: the kernel will actually WAKE a
    # program up the moment anything is mounted or unmounted, rather than making
    # it ask over and over. `poll` is how you ask to be woken, and POLLPRI is
    # the particular knock this file uses.
    #   Source: Linux kernel Documentation/filesystems/proc.rst — "the mountinfo
    #   file ... can be monitored with poll()/epoll(), which returns POLLPRI when
    #   the mount table changes"
    #
    # The two-second timeout on the wait is not polling for drives — that part is
    # genuinely event-driven. It is so that we also notice a NEW FILE appearing
    # on the desktop, which is the other half of this program's job. All it does
    # on each tick is ask the desktop folder "what time were you last changed?",
    # which is a single, very cheap question, and only if the answer is new does
    # it look any further.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    try:
        watcher = open("/proc/self/mountinfo", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"cannot watch for drives being mounted ({error}); giving up")
        return 1

    poller = select.poll()
    poller.register(watcher.fileno(), select.POLLPRI | select.POLLERR)

    try:
        while True:
            events = poller.poll(2000)

            if events:
                # Something was mounted or unmounted. Read the file to clear the
                # kernel's "there is news" flag, then redo the icons.
                watcher.seek(0)
                watcher.read()
                # A moment's pause: a drive being mounted is several steps, and
                # waiting lets them finish so we read one settled answer instead
                # of three half-finished ones.
                time.sleep(0.4)
                run_once(desktop)
                last_desktop_change = _mtime(desktop)
                continue

            changed = _mtime(desktop)
            if changed != last_desktop_change:
                last_desktop_change = changed
                run_once(desktop)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        return 0
    finally:
        watcher.close()


def _mtime(path: str) -> float:
    try:
        return os.stat(path).st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return 0.0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
